<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tinchebray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1106">1106</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tinchebray was fought on 28 September 1106 in south-western Normandy between Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, duke of Normandy. Henry won quickly and captured Robert, who spent the rest of his life in captivity. The victory gave Henry control of Normandy and reunited it with England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tinchebray.jpg" alt="Battle of Tinchebray" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohan Master · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tinchebray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Henry invaded Normandy in 1105, in the course of a long dynastic dispute with his brother. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> but broke off the campaign because of political problems arising from an investiture controversy. With these settled, he returned to Normandy in the spring of 1106, quickly took the fortified abbey of Saint-Pierre-sur-Dives near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falaise">Falaise</a>, and turned south to besiege Tinchebray Castle, on a hill above the town.</p><p>Tinchebray, today in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a> département of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, lay on the border of the county of Mortain in the south-west of Normandy. It was held by William, Count of Mortain, one of the few important Norman barons still loyal to Robert. Duke Robert brought up his forces to break the siege, and after some unsuccessful negotiations he decided that a battle in the open was his best option.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Henry's army was organised in three groups. Ranulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, Robert de Beaumont, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, and William de Warenne, 2nd Earl of Surrey, commanded the two main forces, while a reserve under Elias I of Maine waited out of sight on the flank. Alan IV, Duke of Brittany, William, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>, Ralph of Tosny, Robert of Montfort and Robert of Grandmesnil also fought on Henry's side. William of Mortain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-belleme">Robert of Bellême, 3rd Earl of Shrewsbury</a>, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>.</p><p>The battle lasted an hour. Henry dismounted and ordered most of his knights to do the same, which was unusual in Norman warfare and gave the infantry a decisive part. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> charged the front line with men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a> and the Cotentin, and Henry's reserve decided the day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Most of Robert's army was captured or killed. Among the prisoners were Robert himself, Edgar Ætheling, the uncle of Henry's wife, and William of Mortain. Robert of Bellême, commanding the duke's rear guard, led the retreat and escaped. Most prisoners were released, but Robert and William of Mortain were held for the rest of their lives. Henry kept his brother first in Devizes Castle and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, where Robert died at Cardiff Castle in 1134. Robert's legitimate son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-clito">William Clito</a> (1102–1128), pressed claims to the duchy that fed several rebellions through the rest of Henry's reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tinchebray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later dukes fought for the duchy itself: Henry I of England defeated his brother Robert Curthose at the <b>Battle of Tinchebray</b> in 1106 and Louis VI of France at the Battle of Brémule in 1119.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
